 ## Overview
-Dynamic and Temporal Modeling extends static volumetric representations to handle non-rigid motion and time-varying scenes by parameterizing attributes as functions of time or modeling temporal deformation fields [G]. It solves the limitation of static radiance fields and fixed Gaussian sets, which cannot represent evolving geometry or appearance [G]. It sets itself apart from static methods by introducing explicit temporal dependencies, either through canonical space deformation [13852-deformation-field-modeling.md] or direct time-conditioned attribute prediction [17390-temporal-input-conditioning.md], and distinguishes itself from offline 4D reconstruction methods by supporting on-the-fly or incremental training strategies [7582-on-the-fly-dynamic-nerf-training.md][9203-3dgstream.md].
+Dynamic and Temporal Modeling extends static volumetric representations to handle non-rigid motion and time-varying scenes by parameterizing attributes as functions of time or modeling temporal deformation fields [G]. It solves the limitation of static radiance fields and fixed Gaussian sets, which cannot represent evolving geometry or appearance [G]. It sets itself apart from static methods by introducing explicit temporal dependencies, either through canonical space deformation [Deformation Field Modeling](13852-deformation-field-modeling.md) or direct time-conditioned attribute prediction [Temporal Input Conditioning](17390-temporal-input-conditioning.md), and distinguishes itself from offline 4D reconstruction methods by supporting on-the-fly or incremental training strategies [On-the-Fly Dynamic NeRF Training](7582-on-the-fly-dynamic-nerf-training.md) and [3DGStream](9203-3dgstream.md) [G].
 
 ## Shared principles
 The family shares the following core mechanisms for handling temporal dynamics:
 
-1.  **Temporal Parameterization:** Attributes that vary with time, such as position $\mathbf{c}$, rotation $\mathbf{q}$, opacity $\alpha$, density $\sigma$, or color $\mathbf{c}$, are expressed as functions of time $t$ [13851-blur-and-transient-modeling.md][13852-deformation-field-modeling.md][17390-temporal-input-conditioning.md]. Common functional forms include:
-    *   **Polynomial/Linear:** $\mathbf{q}(t) = \mathbf{q}_0 + \mathbf{m}t$ [13851-blur-and-transient-modeling.md][13852-deformation-field-modeling.md][17390-temporal-input-conditioning.md].
-    *   **Fourier Series:** $\mathbf{c}(t) = \mathbf{c}_0 + \sum_{k=0}^{K} (\mathbf{a}_k \cos(k \omega t) + \mathbf{b}_k \sin(k \omega t))$ [13851-blur-and-transient-modeling.md][13852-deformation-field-modeling.md][17390-temporal-input-conditioning.md].
-    *   **Neural Deformation:** A neural network $\Psi_t(x, t)$ predicts a displacement $\Delta x$ to warp dynamic coordinates to a canonical space [13851-blur-and-transient-modeling.md][13852-deformation-field-modeling.md][17390-temporal-input-conditioning.md].
-    *   **Implicit Fields:** Time-dependent NeRFs map $(x, d, t)$ to density and color, often using local neighborhoods of frames [13851-blur-and-transient-modeling.md][13852-deformation-field-modeling.md][17390-temporal-input-conditioning.md].
