 ## Background
-Dynamic and Temporal Modeling in volumetric rendering originated from the need to extend static Neural Radiance Fields (NeRF) and 3D Gaussian Splatting to 4D scenes [G]. Early works like D-NeRF introduced canonical space deformation for dynamic NeRFs [13852-deformation-field-modeling.md]. The adaptation to 3D Gaussian Splatting followed, with methods like 4D-GS and 3DGStream introducing explicit temporal parameterization and neural transformation caches [17368-4d-gs.md][9203-3dgstream.md]. On-the-fly training methods like OD-NeRF further enabled real-time streaming by leveraging Markov assumptions [7582-on-the-fly-dynamic-nerf-training.md].
+Dynamic and Temporal Modeling in volumetric rendering originated from the need to extend static Neural Radiance Fields (NeRF) and 3D Gaussian Splatting to 4D scenes [G]. Early works like D-NeRF introduced canonical space deformation for dynamic NeRFs [Deformation Field Modeling](13852-deformation-field-modeling.md). The adaptation to 3D Gaussian Splatting followed, with methods like [4D-GS](17368-4d-gs.md) and [3DGStream](9203-3dgstream.md) introducing explicit temporal parameterization and neural transformation caches [G]. On-the-fly training methods like [On-the-Fly Dynamic NeRF Training](7582-on-the-fly-dynamic-nerf-training.md) further enabled real-time streaming by leveraging Markov assumptions [G].
 
